                                "sense_id": "12193e49-6c00-32d1-829d-1753ee538166",
                                "text": "The avoyer in office presided both in the council and senate, in each of which he had no regular, but only a casting vote: the great seal of the republic was in his custody; and a provincial jurisdiction was annexed to his station."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f4e9159b-7bc1-48ff-b7cf-fab046e8a8a5",
                                "sense_id": "12193e49-6c00-32d1-829d-1753ee538166",
                                "text": "1809, Unnamed translator, Lazzaro Spallanzani, Travels in the Two Sicilies and Some Parts of the Apennines, in John Pinkerton, A General Collection of the Best and Most Interesting Voyages and Travels in All Parts of the World, Volume 5, page 717,\nThe reigning avoyer, with a ſceptre in his hand, pronounces an harangue; then delivering up the ſceptre and ſeals, reſigns his dignity, and receives the thanks of the aſſembly, by the mouth of the attorney-general, for his zeal and attention during the year of his government."
